From: Stained Glass OS <ke7oxh@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] wbemprox, wbemdisp, wscript: WMI event queries.

IWbemServices::ExecNotificationQuery failed and ExecNotificationQueryAsync ran the event query
as an ordinary data query, so no program watching WMI events got one (.NET's
ManagementEventWatcher, VBScript NextEvent and SWbemSink, C++ services waiting for a process or a
USB device). Events of classes without an event provider are found as Windows finds them, by
polling (events.c): __InstanceCreation/Deletion/Modification/OperationEvent WITHIN n WHERE
TargetInstance ISA 'Class' [AND conditions on TargetInstance's properties] runs the class's data
query every n seconds (1 without WITHIN) and compares instances by __RELPATH (TargetInstance,
PreviousInstance, TIME_CREATED); Win32_ProcessStartTrace/StopTrace/ProcessTrace come from
Win32_Process, Win32_DeviceChangeEvent from Win32_PnPEntity, Win32_VolumeChangeEvent from
Win32_LogicalDisk (EventType 2/3). Semisynchronous enumerators (WBEM_FLAG_RETURN_IMMEDIATELY |
WBEM_FLAG_FORWARD_ONLY required, as documented; Next waits up to its timeout, WBEM_S_TIMEDOUT),
async sinks until CancelAsyncCall (SetStatus WBEM_E_CALL_CANCELLED), any number per services
object. wbemdisp: SWbemServices.ExecNotificationQueryAsync relays the events to the thread that
asked and fires the SWbemSink's OnObjectReady there, OnCompleted after Cancel; SWbemSink
EnumConnectionPoints/EnumConnections. wscript: WScript.Sleep takes the thread's messages, as
Windows' does, so a script's sink handlers run while it sleeps.
